  daenerys begins the show as the khaleesi  of a wild dothraki horde after losing  and gaining control of these proud horse  lords many times over she eventually  makes her way to westeros with a massive  dothraki army at her back after losing  one of her dragons to the night king  daenerys finds herself at a disadvantage  as the white walkers head towards  westeros as the battle begins seemingly  most if not all of her dothraki warriors  charge and are immediately snuffed out  by the army of the dead however just a  few episodes later after daenerys  overtakes king's landing the camera pans  across what looks like thousands of  dothraki warriors enthralled by her  speech about conquering the world david  benioff himself said that the battle of  winterfell was quote essentially the end  of the dothraki so how does daenerys  have so many horse lords left after that  frigid battle  in the series finale tyrion is given  free reign to choose the next monarch he  decides that bran stark who was quote  the best story should be crowned bran  the broken and rule the seven kingdoms  whether or not bran actually does have  the best story this choice makes little  sense bran has become the inhuman and  omniscient three-eyed raven as he has  made clear he cares about little  including the throne after game of  thrones spent years signaling that a  great leader would be honorable as well  as cunning why does his sister sansa who  is both get a junior queenship while her  brother gets the real prize  game of thrones biggest mystery always  concerned the white walkers an undead  race of beings who live in the far north  beyond the wall the white walkers and  their leader the night king pose an  enormous threat to all of westeros as  the army of the dead will eventually  make its way to the seven kingdoms in  wage war throughout the series viewers  get small pieces of information about  the walkers including that they are  given babies as offerings from humans  who live north of the wall who they turn  into tiny walkers however in the season  8 episode the long night when the  walkers finally make their way to  winterfell arya stark dispatches the  night king without too much trouble and  the mysteries surrounding the white  walkers die with him and his entire army  in the seventh season several of  daenerys allies go on an ill-advised  mission north of the wall to try and  prove to cersei that the white walkers  are real southerners like cersei  consider them to be a myth thus tyrion  proposes capturing a white and bringing  it to show cersei however this plan is  foolish right from the start whites  rarely travel alone so trying to capture  a single white and return it in one  piece is essentially a suicide mission  also cersei is certainly shocked by the  white when she sees it but she wages war  against them anyway and all that  daenerys's suicide squad has to show for  their trouble is a stray white and a fel  dragon  after daenerys swoops in to rescue the  gang she suffers a major loss when the  night king shoots one of her dragons  viserion out of the sky the image of  assyrian's eye reopening with its new  blue hue is definitely one of thrones  coolest visuals but the mechanics of how  the night king and his army retrieve the  dragon from underneath the ice raises  some questions earlier in that same  episode the white capturers keep walkers  at bay by isolating themselves on a rock  surrounded by water as whites can't swim  if that's so how did they retrieve  viserion beyond that the army of the  dead uses enormous chains to drag  viserion to the surface where did they  get those and how did they attach them  to the dragon underwater  on game of thrones one of the most  difficult obstacles is travel for that  reason early seasons of game of thrones  are more or less devoted to long  journeys making it clear that people  like daenerys who is trying to get to  westeros have a long way to go however  as the show continued writers focused  less on westeros's sprawling landscape  to the point where the rules of time and  space seem to stop mattering entirely  journeys that would have once taken a  season and a half eventually only take a  couple of minutes gentry can run from a  mountaintop north of the wall to send a  raven to daenerys who arrives on a  dragon shortly thereafter suspension of  disbelief is fine for a show that  involves dragons and magic but game of  thrones space-time continuum develops  not just a tear but some pretty enormous  holes  melisandre is a cryptic priestess of  roller the lord of light initially  melisandre who serves stannis baratheon  until his death is the only red priest  viewers meet but others eventually  appear beyond their fire related powers  they deliver prophecies especially  melisandre who makes sweeping  proclamations about chosen ones and  rightful kings stannis baratheon  warrior of light your sword awaits you  these prophecies are all exciting in the  beginning but by the time game of  thrones draws to a close none of them  have been fulfilled when all is said and  done melisandre's predictions have  little to no bearing on anything in the  show's outcome and are never explained  at all  brawn is a fan favorite as the beloved  wisecracking mercenary who spends time  with both jaime and tyrion lannister  throughout the series but a bizarre  plotline takes place during the eighth  season in which braun agrees to kill  jaime and tyrion simply because cersei  offered him money he threatens the  brothers with the crossbow then isn't  seen again until the finale by then  tyrion has made him lord of highgarden  which he promised braun in exchange for  his and jaime's lives as well as master  of coin to king bran earlier in the  series brawn says he's never borrowed  money and doesn't know how it works so  making a man who can't figure out the  concept of alone the master of coin  seems unwise  game of thrones is home to plenty of  moments that require viewers 