d the two  fall head over heels for each other  putting their lackluster chemistry aside  as well as the fact that they're  actually on to nephew daenerys risking  her quest for the iron throne over a  lover is wildly inconsistent with her  character it doesn't make sense until  you consider that as a plot device it  forces jon to face some internal  conflict about killing the woman who's  not just his queen but his lover  samwell tarly might be one of game of  thrones most lovable characters but he's  definitely not the toughest fighter  while out in the far north with a few  other men from the night's watch sam and  his black clad brothers hear a horn  sound three times indicating that white  walkers have been spotted while sam's  compatriots make a run for it he can't  catch up and so he hides behind a rock  this leads to a thrilling scene in which  sam watches an entire army of walkers  make their way towards the wall but it  also raises questions not only would he  not be able to get ahead of the army but  a walker atop an undead horse makes  prolonged eye contact with him the white  walkers will kill anything in their path  besides the fact that he's a main  character why is sam any different  when audiences first meet jon snow and  ned stark they're told that jon is ned's  shameful bastard the product of an  affair with a tavern wench but ned  prices his honor above all else making  the situation seem a little shady  ultimately the truth is revealed jon  isn't a bastard at all but is the  legitimate product of ned's sister  lyanna stark and daenerys brother prince  rhaegar targaryen who married in secret  as lyanna laid dying with robert  baratheon's forces approaching she  begged her brother to keep jon's real  identity a secret to save him from  robert's sword this is true enough but  the lengths to which ned goes to keep  this secret are extreme for years ned's  loving wife caitlyn resents jon as  shameful proof of her husband's  infidelity couldn't ned have told his  wife the real story  jon snow has one of the toughest  journeys on game of thrones yet somehow  he ends up with a reasonably happy  ending after helping daenerys conquer  king's landing he realizes that his  queen and lover has turned evil in her  quest for power in the aftermath of her  massacre of king's landing civilians he  stabs her for the good of the realm  though daenerys loyal soldiers are  furious a deal is reached and jon is  banished back to the night's watch  landing him right back where he began  jon is always happiest when he's north  of the wall so at face value this seems  like a perfect ending however there's a  catch the night's watch no longer has  any reason to exist the night's watchmen  protected the wall in the realm from  white walkers but without them there's  nothing for the night's watch to do it's  a neat ending to send jon back to his  brothers in black but it also makes  absolutely no sense  pretty much everything that euron  greyjoy does in the final two seasons of  game of thrones makes no sense as  thrones's final conflict draws near  euron's niece and nephew yara and theon  steal his best ships euron manages to  build 1 000 ships in pretty quick order  and from there he gains perfect aim  overnight taking down one of daenerys  typically impervious dragons with a  perfect shot from a moving ship no less  he's at multiple major disadvantages yet  none of them seem to actually matter in  a show that prides itself on taking  fantasy tropes seriously euron greyjoy  stands out like a lucky sore thumb  out of all the characters on game of  thrones tywin lannister is probably the  smartest man in all of westeros tywin is  a political master who stops at nothing  in his quest to elevate the lannisters  this makes one of his personal  relationships extraordinarily confusing  during the show's second season while  arya is on the run from king's landing  in the wake of her father's execution  she ends up being tywin's cupbearer at  harrenhal the two strike up a solid  rapport especially when tywin realizes  that she's well educated however that  fact alone should have revealed arya's  identity taiwan knows that one of the  two star girls has escaped and arya's  knowledge betrays her highborn status  almost immediately tywin may be the  cleverest man in westeros but apparently  even his considerable brain has its  limits  it takes daenerys until the start of  thrones's seventh season to arrive in  westeros she makes a triumphant return  to dragonstone her ancestral home in a  near silent scene that closes the  seventh season's premiere daenerys  strides across the shore she barely  remembers at last she's finally ready to  take on her westerosi foes however  there's one thing about this scene that  doesn't add up after stannis deaths why  doesn't anyone acquainted with the  lannister forces even bother trying to  take dragonstone as a coastal stronghold  it would have been wise for daenerys  enemies to send even just a few soldiers  to protect the place where she was born  especially because they likely knew she  would return  cersei might be a vicious power player  throughout thrones but she does truly  love her children watching her lose her  two younger children in incredibly  tragic ways almost makes you pity the  villainous  she was good  from her first breath she was so sweet  i don't know where she came from  however we know long before her last  child dies that losing children was her  destiny in the fifth season's premiere a  young cersei meets a witch who tells her  that she will have three children and  that their crowns and shrouds will both  be golden yet in the show's second  episode cersei tells caitlyn stark that  she once had a brown-haired baby  presumably the baby she had with robert  baratheon that would mean she had four  children making this otherwise correct  prophecy suddenly null and void in the  series's final episode bran is chosen as  the next king of the seven kin